      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High GI carbohydrate examples include foods like white bread, potato crisps, and sugary cereals. Low GI carbohydrate examples are whole grains like oatmeal, brown rice, legumes, and most vegetables. The degree to which GI affects blood sugar control can vary, but it's generally thought to be more significant when paired with a high carbohydrate diet. By focusing on low GI carbohydrates, individuals generally have a more stable blood sugar reading, which can help prevent health issues related to excessive insulin spikes and crashes.",
      "source": "target",
